<meta type=article>
  <title | Enscribe Authoring Guide>
  <author | The Enscribe Project>
</meta>

This is the reference you keep open while writing. It answers "how do I do X" —
the exact syntax, the available arguments, the edge cases — for every element
Enscribe offers. It assumes you have done the
<a href="quickstart.html" | Quickstart> (or have equivalent familiarity) and
understand Enscribe's three-layer idea: most elements can be written three ways —
a **canonical** named tag, a **sigil** shorthand, and a **markdown** idiom — and
all of them compile to the same Layer 1 HTML. Where three forms exist, this guide
shows all three; where an element has only one form, it says so.

Each section is self-contained: you can read "Inline elements" without reading
anything before it. Jump to what you need. This first installment covers the four
foundational chapters — document structure, sections, inline elements, and block
elements. The remaining chapters are listed at the end.

<# #sec:structure | Chapter 1. Document structure #>

Every Enscribe document opens with a `<meta>` block declaring its type, and may
carry a `<config>` block of document-level settings. These are *apparatus* — they
describe the document rather than belong to its body — so by convention `<meta>`
sits at the very top and `<config>` (with `<data>`) sits at the end.

**The `<meta>` block.** `<meta>` carries the document's identity and front
matter. Its `type` attribute picks the document shape — `article` (flat) or
`book` (chaptered):

```
<meta type=article>
  <title | A Short Account of Calibration>
  <author | Dana Okonkwo>
  <date | 2026-05-31>
</meta>
```

The child tags — `<title>`, `<author>`, `<date>` — are written in **pipe form**
(`<tag | content>`). `<meta>` and its children are canonical-only: there is no
sigil or markdown shorthand for them, because document metadata is explicit by
nature. The block renders into the document's front matter (`<article-front>` for
an article, `<book-front>` for a book), and `<title>` becomes the document title
(`<article-title>` or `<book-title>` accordingly). A document may carry more front
matter than this — abstracts, affiliations — but title, author, and date are the
core three.

**Article versus book.** `type=article` produces a flat document: body content,
then back matter. `type=book` expects chapters and parts and produces a chaptered
document. The choice is structural, not cosmetic — it changes how counters reset
(figures and equations number within each chapter in a book), where footnotes
collect, and where the bibliography lands. Book structure has its own chapter
(forthcoming); for a journal-style article, `type=article` is what you want.

**The `<config>` block.** `<config>` sets document-level options as keyword
attributes. It is apparatus, so it goes at the end of the document:

```
<config citation-style="apa" number-figures=false>
</config>
```

The settings that are live today control citations, numbering, and scoping:
`citation-style` (the CSL style name passed to the citation engine);
`number-equations`, `number-figures`, `number-tables`, `number-theorems`,
`number-definitions`, `number-examples` (set any to `false` to suppress that
counter); `counter-reset-scope` and `note-scope` (how counters and footnotes
reset in books); and any `ref-prefix-*` key (to customize cross-reference
prefixes). Further keys — `theme`, `display-style`, and others — are reserved:
accepted but not yet consumed.

> **Gotcha — `<config>` is not the rendering API.** Options like
> `embedResources` and `dslMode` are passed to the renderer when you process a
> document (covered in the Rendering chapter, forthcoming); they are *not*
> `<config>` keys. A kwarg `<config>` does not recognize is dropped with a console
> warning rather than rendered, so a misplaced `embedResources=false` inside
> `<config>` silently does nothing.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:sections" | Sections and headings> (Chapter 2);
book structure and the `<data>`/`<library>` block are covered in later chapters.

<# #sec:sections | Chapter 2. Sections and headings #>

Section headings divide the body. Enscribe has three heading levels, each
available in all three forms, and all forms compile to the same Layer 1
`<section>` (or `<sub-section>`, `<sub-sub-section>`) wrapping a matching title
element.

**The three forms, side by side.** These three lines produce identical output —
a `<section>` titled "Methods":

```
<# Methods #>           sigil form: the fewest keystrokes
<section | Methods>     canonical form: explicit; what the others become
# Methods               markdown form: a single leading hash
```

The level is the number of hashes, and it is the same across the sigil and
markdown forms:

```
<# Title #>     # Title       →  <section>           (level 1)
<## Title ##>   ## Title       →  <sub-section>       (level 2)
<### Title ###> ### Title      →  <sub-sub-section>   (level 3)
```

The sigil form (`<# … #>`) is the recommended default for most authoring: it is
the lightest to type and still carries ids and attributes. The canonical
`<section>` is the explicit form — exactly what you would read if you converted
the finished HTML back to shorthand. The markdown `#` is there so muscle memory
works on day one; it lifts to the canonical section automatically, but it is a
*lossy* convenience — it has nowhere to put an id, so Layer 1 never round-trips
back to it.

**Section ids.** Give a section an id when something will cross-reference it, by
placing `#name` before the pipe:

```
<# #sec:methods | Methods #>
```

This renders `<section id="sec:methods">`, which a `<ref>` or an
`<a href="#sec:methods" | …>` link can target. Ids on sections use the same `#id`
syntax used everywhere else in Enscribe.

**Deeper headings.** The ladder stops at three section levels on purpose — three
is what a journal article needs. A fourth-level markdown heading and beyond do not
create sections; they pass through as plain HTML heading elements:

```
#### Subhead       →  <h4>Subhead</h4>
##### Subhead       →  <h5>Subhead</h5>
```

These are ordinary headings with no sectioning semantics, no id slot, and no role
in numbering. If a document genuinely needs to nest deeper than three levels, it
usually wants to be a *book* — chapters and parts, a different top-level shape —
rather than a fourth heading level. In books, sections number automatically within
each chapter; the details are in the cross-references and book-structure chapters.

> **Gotcha — hash count is the level, not the importance.** In ordinary Markdown
> `#` is the document's top title. In Enscribe the title comes from `<meta>`, so
> `#` is the first *body* section, `##` a sub-section, `###` a sub-sub-section.
> Match the sigil hash count to the markdown hash count: `<# … #>` and `# …` are
> both level 1.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:structure" | Document structure> (Chapter 1);
cross-references and book numbering are covered in later chapters.

<# #sec:inline | Chapter 3. Inline elements #>

Inline elements format a run of text inside a paragraph. The formatting tags each
support two Enscribe spellings — **same-line long form** (`<b>bold</b>`) and
**pipe form** (`<b | bold>`) — and both produce the same output, so use whichever
reads better. Bold, italic, and strikethrough additionally have markdown idioms.

**Bold and italic.** These have familiar markdown forms most authors will reach
for; the tags are for when you want the element explicit:

```
**bold**   <b>bold</b>    <b | bold>      →  <b>bold</b>
*italic*   <i>italic</i>  <i | italic>    →  <i>italic</i>
```

Markdown `_underscores_` also produce italic, identically to `*asterisks*`.

**Strikethrough, underline, quotation.** Strikethrough has a markdown form
(`~~text~~`); underline and inline quotation do not, so use the tag:

```
~~struck~~   <s>struck</s>   <s | struck>     →  <s>struck</s>
             <u>under</u>    <u | under>      →  <u>under</u>
             <q>quoted</q>   <q | quoted>     →  <q>quoted</q>
```

`<q>` is HTML's inline-quotation element (a browser renders it with quotation
marks); reserve it for short in-line quotes, and use a blockquote (Chapter 4) for
anything that stands on its own.

**Inline code.** Backticks are the markdown form, and a sigil form exists for
symmetry with the other sigils:

```
`code()`        <` code() `>      →  <code>code()</code>
```

Backticks are almost always what you want. Content inside inline code is verbatim —
no idioms or tags are interpreted within it.

**Inline math.** Mathematics inside a run of text uses LaTeX dollar signs, handed
to KaTeX <cite @katex>; the sigil form is the canonical spelling:

```
$\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$        <$ \varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2 $>
```

Both render the same selectable, scalable HTML math. Everything between the
delimiters is LaTeX, passed through verbatim.

**Superscript and subscript.** Brace-delimited sigils set super- and subscripts:

```
x^{2}     →  x<sup>2</sup>
H_{2}O    →  H<sub>2</sub>O
```

The braces are required — `x^2` without them is literal text. These compose, so
`x^{y_{1}}` nests a subscript inside a superscript.

**Links.** Enscribe uses the `<a>` tag, not the markdown `[text](url)` form (which
Enscribe does not support). The URL is the first positional argument, or the
`href` keyword; the text is the body:

```
<a https://example.com | Example>
<a href="https://example.com" | Example>
<a href="https://example.com">Example</a>   →  <a href="https://example.com">Example</a>
```

> **Gotcha — when to use `href=`.** The bare positional URL is fine for ordinary
> URLs, but a URL that is *just* a fragment (`#sec:methods`) collides with the
> `#id` syntax, and a URL containing spaces or a `>` will end the tag early. In
> those cases use the quoted `href` keyword form:
> `<a href="#sec:methods" | jump to Methods>`.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:block" | Block elements> (Chapter 4) for display math and
code blocks; cross-references (which link by name rather than URL) are a later
chapter.

<# #sec:block | Chapter 4. Block elements #>

Block elements stand on their own between paragraphs.

**Paragraphs** are implicit, exactly as in Markdown <cite @commonmark>: write
prose, and separate paragraphs with a blank line. There is no paragraph tag to
type in ordinary writing.

**Blockquotes.** The markdown `>` works; the canonical `<blockquote>` works
same-line or multi-line:

```
> A quoted line.

<blockquote>A quoted line.</blockquote>

<blockquote | A quoted line.>
```

The markdown form wraps its content in a paragraph (`<blockquote><p>…</p>`); the
tag forms with inline content do not. A blockquote's content is ordinary
Enscribe — formatting, math, and references inside it are interpreted normally.

**Code blocks.** A fenced block is the standard form; add a language name after
the opening fence for syntax highlighting. The triple-backtick *sigil* is the
canonical equivalent. Both appear below — the outer fence here is four backticks
so the three-backtick examples inside it display literally:

````
```python
def f(x):
    return x + 1
```

<``` python | def f(x): return x + 1 ```>
````

Both render `<pre><code class="language-python">…</code></pre>`. Code-block content
is verbatim — no idioms or tags are interpreted inside it — and the block can take
an id (an `#code:f` token after the language) for cross-referencing. There is no
`<code-block>` named tag to author directly; the fenced block and the
triple-backtick sigil are the two forms.

**Display math.** Mathematics that deserves its own centered, numbered line is
*display* math. Write it as a `$$` fence with the delimiters on their own lines,
or with the canonical doubled-dollar sigil:

```
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi}
$$

<$$ | \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi} $$>
```

Like a figure, display math can take an id (`<$$ #eqn:gauss | … $$>`) so prose can
refer back to it, and it is numbered automatically.

> **Gotcha — `$$x$$` on one line is *inline*, not display.** The `$$` fence only
> produces display math when the delimiters are on their own lines. A single-line
> `$$x = y$$` renders as inline math. If you want display math without a multi-line
> fence, use the sigil `<$$ | x = y $$>`, which is always display regardless of
> line placement.

**Math environments.** LaTeX-style multi-line math environments are available as
tags: `<align>`, `<eqnarray>`, `<cases>`, and `<matrix>`. They carry verbatim
LaTeX and render through KaTeX as display blocks:

```
<align>
a &= b + c \\
d &= e + f
</align>
```

Use the long form (opener and closer on their own lines) for multi-row content;
the pipe form works for short cases. The aligned block is numbered like other
display math.

*Related:* <a href="#sec:inline" | Inline elements> (Chapter 3) for inline math
and inline code; figures and tables are the next chapter to land.

<# What's next #>

This installment covers document structure, sections, inline elements, and block
elements. Chapters on **figures and images**, **tables**, **citations and
bibliography**, **footnotes**, **cross-references**, the **theorem family**,
**external DSLs** (Mermaid, ABC), **book structure**, **arguments and the pipe**,
and **rendering and output** are coming in subsequent updates. For the
architecture behind the canonical-form model, read the
<a href="design.html" | Design article>; for a guided tour of the whole feature
set in one page, see the <a href="quickstart.html" | Quickstart>.

